🧭 When to use .loc
Use .loc when you want to select data by label — meaning:

A row index label (e.g., 'ATL', 'row_42', or even 42 if that is the index label)

A column name (e.g., 'Code', 'Description')

A boolean mask (e.g., df['Description'] == airport_name)

.loc is about meaning, not position.

Examples
python
df.loc[42, 'Code']          # row with index label 42, column 'Code'
df.loc[:, 'Description']    # all rows, column 'Description'
df.loc[df['Code'] > 10000]  # boolean mask


🎯 When to use .iloc
Use .iloc when you want to select data by integer position — meaning:

The first row → .iloc[0]

The third column → .iloc[:, 2]

The last row → .iloc[-1]

.iloc is about location, not label.

Examples
python
df.iloc[0, 1]   # first row, second column
df.iloc[:, 0]   # all rows, first column
df.iloc[-1]     # last row



🧠 A simple rule of thumb
Use this	When you want to select by…	Example
.loc	label (row index, column name, boolean mask)	df.loc['ATL'], df.loc[df['Code'] > 10000]
.iloc	position (0th row, 1st column, last row)	df.iloc[0], df.iloc[-1]
